  ],
  "solution": "<p><strong>1.</strong> Write the three coefficients as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mi>n</mi><mi>r</mi></mfrac><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Their consecutive ratios are 2 and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, not 2 and 3.</p><p><strong>2.</strong> Cancelling factorials gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></math>. The first says <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>; the second says <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>5</mn><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>3.</strong> Substitution gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>14</mn></mrow></math>, so F. Check: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mrow><mn>14</mn></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1001</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mrow><mn>14</mn></mrow><mn>5</mn></mfrac><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2002</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mrow><mn>14</mn></mrow><mn>6</mn></mfrac><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3003</mn></mrow></math>, whose ratio is indeed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi>:</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>:</mi><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>.</p>"
